    {
      "id": "HCR-03",
      "chapter": "lifts",
      "anchor": "lifts-isotropic-shell-volume",
      "title": "A finite arithmetic factor bounds every shell of the isotropic-line stabilizer",
      "statement": "In Hermitian dimension s+2 over the CM field E, the parabolic stabilizing an isotropic line has measurable shells covering modulo its rational points with mass ≤K exp(−2d(s+1)j), j∈ℤ, d=[F:ℚ]. The fixed finite factor is the product of the volumes of two compact additive sets, one lower-dimensional arithmetic quotient, the multiplicative measure of a compact idèle set and its supremum of the continuous modulus character.",
      "hypotheses": "E/F CM. A hyperbolic pair is fixed. The complement is nondegenerate, with finite arithmetic volume in every Haar normalization. Haar measures on the additive coordinates and idèles are fixed, and the real flow scales all archimedean components and is the identity at finite places. The downstream geometric count has s=M−2≥1.",
      "correspondence": "The product and the almost-everywhere covering are explicit. The formal source supplies finite covolume by its own separate arithmetic construction. Borel’s adelic finiteness theorem, with the check of rational characters for a unitary group, gives a classical proof of the same input. The idèle norm-one compactness admits both a classical source and the archive’s independent compactness argument.",
      "dependencies": [],
      "equations": [
        "lifts-isotropic-shell-product"
      ],
